---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Common Overtime Misconceptions | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'The most frequent misunderstandings about overtime pay: salaried workers, "comp time", weekend premiums, and more.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Common Overtime Misconceptions</h1>
  <p>Both workers and employers frequently get overtime wrong. The most common mistaken beliefs:</p>

  <h2>"Salaried employees can't get overtime"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Being paid a salary doesn't automatically make you exempt. Exemption requires BOTH a salary at or above the federal threshold AND meeting the duties test for executive, administrative, professional, or specific other categories. Many salaried employees are still entitled to overtime.</p>

  <h2>"My boss called me a manager so I'm exempt"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Job titles don't determine exemption. What you actually do does. If your primary duty is non-managerial work (operating a register, taking orders, processing data), you may be entitled to overtime even with a "manager" or "supervisor" title.</p>

  <h2>"Weekend hours always count as overtime"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong (federally).</strong> Federal overtime is based on hours over 40 in a workweek, not on which day. If you work 32 hours Monday-Friday and 8 hours Saturday, that's 40 total &mdash; no overtime federally. Some union contracts and a few state industries do require weekend premiums.</p>

  <h2>"My employer can give me comp time instead"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong for private-sector employees.</strong> The FLSA doesn't allow private employers to substitute compensatory time off for overtime pay. Public-sector employees can sometimes receive comp time at 1.5 hours per overtime hour, but with restrictions.</p>

  <h2>"If I agree to work without overtime, that's binding"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Overtime rights under the FLSA can't be waived by agreement. Even a signed contract saying you waive overtime is unenforceable. (Employers sometimes ask employees to sign these anyway &mdash; the document is meaningless.)</p>

  <h2>"My break time is unpaid so I can't claim it"</h2>
  <p><strong>Depends.</strong> Short breaks (5&ndash;20 minutes) are typically paid and count as work. Bona fide meal breaks (30+ minutes where you're completely relieved of duties) can be unpaid. But if you were actually working during your "lunch" &mdash; checking emails, answering calls, monitoring something &mdash; that time is compensable.</p>

  <h2>"Work I did from home doesn't count"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> If you were authorized or expected to do work, the time counts toward your hours, including remote work. Off-the-clock work that your employer knew or should have known about must be compensated.</p>

  <h2>"My pay was correct because they paid me 'time and a half' for my overtime hours"</h2>
  <p><strong>Maybe wrong.</strong> Time-and-a-half is on your REGULAR RATE, which includes non-discretionary bonuses, commissions, and shift differentials &mdash; not just your base hourly wage. If you receive supplemental pay and your overtime was calculated only on base wage, you may be underpaid.</p>

  <h2>"If I sue, I'll just get my back wages"</h2>
  <p><strong>You can often get more.</strong> Under federal FLSA, willful violations entitle you to "liquidated damages" equal to the unpaid wages &mdash; so double back pay. Plus attorney's fees in most cases.</p>
</BaseLayout>
